WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.660
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:05.260
RAM RAMANATHAN:
Welcome, everybody.

00:00:05.260 --> 00:00:07.220
My name is Ram Ramanathan.

00:00:07.220 --> 00:00:11.120
And with me is Rajiv Salimath
from PricewaterhouseCoopers.

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:13.050
What we want to spend
the next hour or so

00:00:13.050 --> 00:00:14.580
is talk to you
guys about how you

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:16.740
can use machine learning
to kind of change

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:18.720
common retail processes.

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:21.500
Have you ever wondered how
we can enable customers

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:24.150
to find the most relevant
and the right product

00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:26.310
at the right time or
how you can enable

00:00:26.310 --> 00:00:30.209
them to search easier than just
doing the data textual query?

00:00:30.209 --> 00:00:32.000
Like, that people are
more and more visual,

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:33.820
how do we enable them
to search better?

00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:35.530
And that's really what we
wanted to spend some time

00:00:35.530 --> 00:00:37.890
over the next hour talking
about how we can apply ML

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:42.190
for common scenarios in retail.

00:00:42.190 --> 00:00:44.280
Here is a lovely diagram
of showing how we

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:45.690
do a deep learning neural net.

00:00:45.690 --> 00:00:47.273
The rest of the
session is going to be

00:00:47.273 --> 00:00:48.840
talking about
convolution neural nets

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:51.170
and spending a lot of
time in deep learning.

00:00:51.170 --> 00:00:52.660
And the goal for
it is, of course,

00:00:52.660 --> 00:00:56.400
to detect whether an
image is a cat or a dog,

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:59.160
which is great for
my vision scenario,

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:02.290
but what does that
have to do with retail?

00:01:02.290 --> 00:01:04.810
The fact that you can find a
cat or a dog is interesting--

00:01:04.810 --> 00:01:06.810
I love dogs-- but
does it really help

00:01:06.810 --> 00:01:08.611
you guys on a day to day basis?

00:01:08.611 --> 00:01:10.110
And that's really
what we want to do

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:12.870
is spend some time thinking
about common ML scenarios

00:01:12.870 --> 00:01:15.330
that you can think of in
your customers among your day

00:01:15.330 --> 00:01:18.730
to day use and how
you can apply that.

00:01:18.730 --> 00:01:20.780
We think of three
key scenarios where

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:22.230
machine learning comes to play.

00:01:22.230 --> 00:01:25.380
One is enabling your
customers or your users

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:27.900
to find the right product
at the right time, right?

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:29.910
How do we take people,
moving them away

00:01:29.910 --> 00:01:31.930
from just regular
textual searches

00:01:31.930 --> 00:01:35.070
to thinking about finding
products more visually?

00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:36.540
The other aspect
is how do we help

00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:39.520
them recommend the right product
at the right time, right?

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:42.120
If you really think
about this, one

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:45.410
of the things that we deal
with more and more is choice.

00:01:45.410 --> 00:01:47.820
And the thing is we can help
customers is, how do you

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:50.250
give them the right product
at the right time based

00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:52.430
on their prior use behavior?

00:01:52.430 --> 00:01:54.460
And the third piece
is, once you have

00:01:54.460 --> 00:01:56.840
released a product
out into the wild,

00:01:56.840 --> 00:01:58.110
how is this product doing?

00:01:58.110 --> 00:01:59.592
What are people
talking about it?

00:01:59.592 --> 00:02:02.050
What else are they referring
to when they use this product?

00:02:02.050 --> 00:02:05.140
So you guys can get a
better feeling of sentiment,

00:02:05.140 --> 00:02:07.230
understanding what they
do with the product,

00:02:07.230 --> 00:02:09.919
and get a better answer that
can then drive your life

00:02:09.919 --> 00:02:12.210
cycle from a product development
perspective, marketing

00:02:12.210 --> 00:02:17.422
perspective, and multiple
downstream activities.

00:02:17.422 --> 00:02:19.630
So I'm going to talk about
a couple, three scenarios.

00:02:19.630 --> 00:02:22.030
So let's talk about why
finding their favorite product.

00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:23.030
How do we do that today?

00:02:23.030 --> 00:02:25.070
Today, most retail
stores, we normally

00:02:25.070 --> 00:02:26.960
think about their
online web presence.

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:28.550
It's normal, you
have a textual box.

00:02:28.550 --> 00:02:30.850
They do a product search query.

00:02:30.850 --> 00:02:33.920
And people normally kind of
gravitate to search first.

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:36.050
Part of it is because
of their behavior

00:02:36.050 --> 00:02:38.646
that they do every day when
they open up a Chrome browser

00:02:38.646 --> 00:02:39.770
and they search in the tab.

00:02:39.770 --> 00:02:42.920
And they kind of got
indoctrinated to search first.

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:45.680
So in those kind of scenarios,
how was your search experience?

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:47.605
And so far, searches
have been mostly textual.

00:02:47.605 --> 00:02:49.730
But one of the things that
we've seen more and more

00:02:49.730 --> 00:02:52.880
is people are moving more
and more to images and video

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:54.620
to kind of communicate, right?

00:02:54.620 --> 00:02:56.810
Whether it's messaging
applications,

00:02:56.810 --> 00:02:59.390
whether it's people pinning
their favorite images

00:02:59.390 --> 00:03:01.730
on Pinterest or on their
favorite social app.

00:03:01.730 --> 00:03:04.640
Or even if you think about
scanning through blogs,

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:07.160
get inspiration to what
they want to purchase.

00:03:07.160 --> 00:03:08.750
Once they do get
the inspiration,

00:03:08.750 --> 00:03:09.890
what do they do with that?

00:03:09.890 --> 00:03:11.390
They can't really
find their product

00:03:11.390 --> 00:03:13.290
at their favorite
retailer anymore,

00:03:13.290 --> 00:03:14.930
because it's an
image, most probably.

00:03:14.930 --> 00:03:16.430
So how do we enable
those scenarios?

00:03:16.430 --> 00:03:20.150
How do you enable your consumers
to discover products easier?

00:03:20.150 --> 00:03:22.352
That's one aspect.

00:03:22.352 --> 00:03:23.810
The second piece
is how do we think

00:03:23.810 --> 00:03:27.020
about recommending the right
product to the right customer?

00:03:27.020 --> 00:03:29.920
I think one of the use cases
when we think about Google

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:32.900
and recommendation is we
have over a billion hours

00:03:32.900 --> 00:03:35.391
of video content watched
every day on YouTube.

00:03:35.391 --> 00:03:37.640
And you may say, OK, what's
that got a recommendation?

00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:39.650
The reason people watch
so much video on YouTube

00:03:39.650 --> 00:03:42.500
is because they see this Related
Video tab on the lower right

00:03:42.500 --> 00:03:43.700
corner.

00:03:43.700 --> 00:03:47.300
Based on your user behavior,
based on your product metadata,

00:03:47.300 --> 00:03:49.300
we then serve up
appropriate videos that's

00:03:49.300 --> 00:03:50.870
relevant for your context.

00:03:50.870 --> 00:03:53.600
Not just the clickbait, meaning
that if you watch a cat video,

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:56.196
show more cat videos, but
really focus on what's

00:03:56.196 --> 00:03:57.320
relevant for that end user.

00:03:57.320 --> 00:03:59.165
And thinking about time
to view and saying,

00:03:59.165 --> 00:04:01.340
is this is a video you
really want to look at?

00:04:01.340 --> 00:04:02.990
That's a case of a
content perspective.

00:04:02.990 --> 00:04:04.781
But you can then
translate that to products

00:04:04.781 --> 00:04:06.030
on a day-to-day basis.

00:04:06.030 --> 00:04:08.030
Just because somebody
bought something last year

00:04:08.030 --> 00:04:09.863
does not mean they're
going to buy something

00:04:09.863 --> 00:04:13.536
this year, because maybe
externalities like seasonality.

00:04:13.536 --> 00:04:14.660
There may be social events.

00:04:14.660 --> 00:04:17.740
Because one of their favorite
teams won their sports event,

00:04:17.740 --> 00:04:19.043
that may drive behavior.

00:04:19.043 --> 00:04:20.459
There may be other
things that are

00:04:20.459 --> 00:04:23.000
more internal factors that drive
recommendations, [INAUDIBLE]

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:24.830
out of stock, et cetera.

00:04:24.830 --> 00:04:26.730
And how do you think
about taking all of that

00:04:26.730 --> 00:04:28.771
to drive the right product
recommendation for end

00:04:28.771 --> 00:04:30.850
customer?

00:04:30.850 --> 00:04:33.350
And the third piece, as I talked
about, is sentiment, right?

00:04:33.350 --> 00:04:36.200
Really now that you release a
product, it's out in the wild,

00:04:36.200 --> 00:04:38.900
people are doing all kinds of
craziness with your product.

00:04:38.900 --> 00:04:41.480
But then what's going
on with the product?

00:04:41.480 --> 00:04:43.220
Do people actually
like it, right?

00:04:43.220 --> 00:04:44.510
I mean, it's funny enough.

00:04:44.510 --> 00:04:48.680
I am a frequent customer of both
Google Shopping and at Amazon.

00:04:48.680 --> 00:04:50.960
And I spend lot of time
reading those reviews.

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:54.630
Those reviews clearly
influence what I purchase.

00:04:54.630 --> 00:04:57.759
And so thinking about for
USA manufacturer or retailer,

00:04:57.759 --> 00:04:59.175
you really need
to understand what

00:04:59.175 --> 00:05:01.592
are people saying about this
product out in the wild.

00:05:01.592 --> 00:05:03.800
And you really want to
understand, what else are they

00:05:03.800 --> 00:05:05.549
talking when they talk
about this product?

00:05:05.549 --> 00:05:07.090
What are the other entities?

00:05:07.090 --> 00:05:09.520
Who's the most
perceived substitute

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:10.654
in the customer's mind?

00:05:10.654 --> 00:05:12.320
What else would they
normally talk about

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:13.450
from a comparative
perspective when

00:05:13.450 --> 00:05:14.690
they talk about your product?

00:05:14.690 --> 00:05:16.364
How can we help you
understand that?

00:05:16.364 --> 00:05:17.780
And that's normally,
all that data

00:05:17.780 --> 00:05:20.750
is not necessarily
inside your organization.

00:05:20.750 --> 00:05:23.600
A lot of data is outside your
organization in social media.

00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:26.060
How do you then harness all
that data to get insights

00:05:26.060 --> 00:05:29.280
into your recommendation?

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:30.710
So that's really
what we're going

00:05:30.710 --> 00:05:32.668
to spend the rest of the
time about these three

00:05:32.668 --> 00:05:35.686
scenarios-- finding products,
talking about recommendations,

00:05:35.686 --> 00:05:37.310
talking about
sentiments, and how do we

00:05:37.310 --> 00:05:39.067
start driving scenarios.

00:05:39.067 --> 00:05:41.150
Here is a quick view of
all the different services

00:05:41.150 --> 00:05:42.816
that we provide on
Google Cloud Platform

00:05:42.816 --> 00:05:44.150
to really think about that.

00:05:44.150 --> 00:05:45.530
And what I want to spend
some time with today--

00:05:45.530 --> 00:05:47.154
how do we bring these
services together

00:05:47.154 --> 00:05:49.300
for one or two scenarios?

00:05:49.300 --> 00:05:51.380
From a machine
learning perspective,

00:05:51.380 --> 00:05:55.910
in general, there are two key
ways that we provide access

00:05:55.910 --> 00:05:57.420
to machine learning.

00:05:57.420 --> 00:05:59.480
One is as a platform play.

00:05:59.480 --> 00:06:01.962
So that's really where
we have clearly--

00:06:01.962 --> 00:06:03.920
we're going to be talking
more about TensorFlow

00:06:03.920 --> 00:06:06.200
tomorrow, where we
think about TensorFlow

00:06:06.200 --> 00:06:08.670
as the number one open source
machine learning platform.

00:06:08.670 --> 00:06:12.440
But any of you can build your
own cloud models on your data,

00:06:12.440 --> 00:06:13.232
run it anywhere.

00:06:13.232 --> 00:06:14.690
And then when you
want, when you're

00:06:14.690 --> 00:06:16.790
ready to run it at scale
in a managed environment,

00:06:16.790 --> 00:06:18.374
you can bring it to our cloud.

00:06:18.374 --> 00:06:19.790
And we have cloud
machine learning

00:06:19.790 --> 00:06:22.700
engine that helps you really
drive your machine learning

00:06:22.700 --> 00:06:25.400
models, both from a training
perspective or prediction

00:06:25.400 --> 00:06:26.182
perspective.

00:06:26.182 --> 00:06:28.640
And that's really targeted at
data scientists and folks who

00:06:28.640 --> 00:06:30.848
want to really manage the
machine learning life cycle

00:06:30.848 --> 00:06:32.690
from end-to-end perspective.

00:06:32.690 --> 00:06:35.390
At the other end is really what
we talk about machine learning

00:06:35.390 --> 00:06:36.440
APIs.

00:06:36.440 --> 00:06:38.780
So these are APIs
that's really focused

00:06:38.780 --> 00:06:42.830
around using Google models,
using Google infrastructure,

00:06:42.830 --> 00:06:45.920
and providing you targeted
solutions for scenarios.

00:06:45.920 --> 00:06:48.110
For example, our own
language to help you

00:06:48.110 --> 00:06:49.970
do things like finding
named entities,

00:06:49.970 --> 00:06:52.010
meaning entities
in a block of text,

00:06:52.010 --> 00:06:53.990
or finding things
like sentiment.

00:06:53.990 --> 00:06:55.280
Whether it's an image--

00:06:55.280 --> 00:06:57.020
we released Vision
API last year.

00:06:57.020 --> 00:06:58.760
So how do you find
common entities?

00:06:58.760 --> 00:07:01.830
And in the case of video,
we just announced yesterday.

00:07:01.830 --> 00:07:04.070
So really, that's where
we're using Google models

00:07:04.070 --> 00:07:06.570
to try to get insight
into unstructured data.

00:07:06.570 --> 00:07:08.970
And we also have other
APIs like Jobs API, where

00:07:08.970 --> 00:07:11.730
we're focused on very
targeted vertical use cases,

00:07:11.730 --> 00:07:13.592
like thinking about
job placement.

00:07:13.592 --> 00:07:16.050
And that's really kind of the
portfolio of machine learning

00:07:16.050 --> 00:07:18.170
products that we have
today from Google.

00:07:18.170 --> 00:07:20.100
Platform, on one hand,
based on TensorFlow

00:07:20.100 --> 00:07:23.690
with Cloud ML Engine and
a set of ready-to-use APIs

00:07:23.690 --> 00:07:25.299
on the ML API side.

00:07:25.299 --> 00:07:26.840
And the goal for
this talk is talking

00:07:26.840 --> 00:07:29.090
about how can we use
some of this technology

00:07:29.090 --> 00:07:30.740
to drive, to go
back to those three

00:07:30.740 --> 00:07:32.060
scenarios we talked about?

00:07:32.060 --> 00:07:33.920
How do you find your
products easier?

00:07:33.920 --> 00:07:35.584
How do you help drive
recommendations?

00:07:35.584 --> 00:07:37.250
And the third aspect
is, how do you help

00:07:37.250 --> 00:07:38.854
customers understand sentiment?

00:07:41.460 --> 00:07:43.500
So let's first focus
on the first one.

00:07:43.500 --> 00:07:45.430
How do you help find
the right product?

00:07:45.430 --> 00:07:46.810
So there are a couple of things.

00:07:46.810 --> 00:07:48.650
And the scenario that
I'm going to focus on

00:07:48.650 --> 00:07:50.280
is really around searches.

00:07:50.280 --> 00:07:51.617
Search by image.

00:07:51.617 --> 00:07:53.700
As you can imagine, there
are lots of other pieces

00:07:53.700 --> 00:07:55.850
that you can do from a
find the right product.

00:07:55.850 --> 00:07:58.090
There are things on search
engine optimization.

00:07:58.090 --> 00:08:01.170
There are things about how you
can tune your existing search

00:08:01.170 --> 00:08:03.870
index and tuning better
using [INAUDIBLE] search.

00:08:03.870 --> 00:08:06.202
But in my mind, those
are technologies

00:08:06.202 --> 00:08:08.410
that have already been in
the market for a few years.

00:08:08.410 --> 00:08:10.380
It's kind of a
well-understood process

00:08:10.380 --> 00:08:12.850
of how you think about
search engine optimization.

00:08:12.850 --> 00:08:15.840
How do you drive searches
across your metadata today?

00:08:15.840 --> 00:08:17.007
People have solutions today.

00:08:17.007 --> 00:08:19.173
So what I want to spend
some time thinking about is,

00:08:19.173 --> 00:08:21.340
how do we think about
finding the right product?

00:08:21.340 --> 00:08:22.440
And that there are
a couple of things

00:08:22.440 --> 00:08:23.606
you can go around the thing.

00:08:23.606 --> 00:08:27.965
One is, when a user
of your website

00:08:27.965 --> 00:08:30.990
sends an image as part
of the search box,

00:08:30.990 --> 00:08:32.599
how can I get metadata
on that image?

00:08:32.599 --> 00:08:33.640
What is that image about?

00:08:33.640 --> 00:08:35.429
So I can drive a search process.

00:08:35.429 --> 00:08:38.230
The other aspect is
thinking about logos.

00:08:38.230 --> 00:08:40.049
Is there any product
branding information

00:08:40.049 --> 00:08:42.580
that I can take to
drive certain aspects?

00:08:42.580 --> 00:08:44.940
And then how do I
think about fraud.

00:08:44.940 --> 00:08:47.880
If I'm a marketplace and
different people are posting,

00:08:47.880 --> 00:08:50.489
sellers and buyers, sellers
are posting products

00:08:50.489 --> 00:08:52.530
that they think they're
ready to sell, how do you

00:08:52.530 --> 00:08:55.140
know, as a marketplace owner,
that this is the right product

00:08:55.140 --> 00:08:55.850
or not?

00:08:55.850 --> 00:08:58.280
Is somebody just doing
a phishing exercise,

00:08:58.280 --> 00:09:00.550
where they're taking
some random stock image,

00:09:00.550 --> 00:09:01.980
posting it on the marketplace?

00:09:01.980 --> 00:09:02.970
Is this a valid image?

00:09:02.970 --> 00:09:05.470
And so we're just going to talk
to a couple of ML techniques

00:09:05.470 --> 00:09:08.040
that enable that scenario.

00:09:08.040 --> 00:09:09.970
The first piece
is image metadata.

00:09:09.970 --> 00:09:11.760
This is something we
released last year.

00:09:16.340 --> 00:09:18.460
It really works in more
generalized scenarios.

00:09:18.460 --> 00:09:20.160
So that the information
you get back

00:09:20.160 --> 00:09:23.794
from what we used to call a
Vision API is more generic.

00:09:23.794 --> 00:09:26.210
I would say, a higher level
of categorization information.

00:09:26.210 --> 00:09:29.220
It's not very targeted,
specific information.

00:09:29.220 --> 00:09:32.377
But it can give you things
like it's a leather handbag

00:09:32.377 --> 00:09:33.960
and give you the
color of the handbag.

00:09:33.960 --> 00:09:37.360
And that can give you
metadata to then drive

00:09:37.360 --> 00:09:40.590
on subsequent search.

00:09:40.590 --> 00:09:43.890
So for example, one
of our customers,

00:09:43.890 --> 00:09:47.015
they may be enabling a search
by image feature in their app.

00:09:47.015 --> 00:09:48.390
They want to start
driving things

00:09:48.390 --> 00:09:50.940
like, hey, I want to search
for, say, brown canvas bag.

00:09:50.940 --> 00:09:52.512
So we'll give the color.

00:09:52.512 --> 00:09:53.970
We'll give you the
term it's a bag.

00:09:53.970 --> 00:09:55.620
And potentially, if
it's a canvas bag.

00:09:55.620 --> 00:09:57.030
And then you can then
drive a search index

00:09:57.030 --> 00:09:58.470
based on that
within the metadata

00:09:58.470 --> 00:10:01.480
they collected already.

00:10:01.480 --> 00:10:03.420
Another piece we
talked about is,

00:10:03.420 --> 00:10:05.460
how do we think about
using the web for fraud

00:10:05.460 --> 00:10:06.600
and topical entities?

00:10:06.600 --> 00:10:09.690
What that is is we just
released our new feature

00:10:09.690 --> 00:10:11.260
called Web Detection.

00:10:11.260 --> 00:10:13.410
So that's really
the functionality

00:10:13.410 --> 00:10:15.400
under Google Image Search.

00:10:15.400 --> 00:10:19.380
So what that gives you is if an
image exists on the web, right?

00:10:19.380 --> 00:10:20.760
So that's important point.

00:10:20.760 --> 00:10:23.241
If an image exists on the web.

00:10:23.241 --> 00:10:25.050
And our web crawler,
as you can imagine,

00:10:25.050 --> 00:10:27.630
we crawl through billions
of pages on a daily basis.

00:10:27.630 --> 00:10:29.520
We index a lot of these images.

00:10:29.520 --> 00:10:32.310
We can then find metadata about
that image and feed that back.

00:10:32.310 --> 00:10:35.550
So when you post an image
to us, we can then tell you,

00:10:35.550 --> 00:10:37.440
this metadata on
this image based

00:10:37.440 --> 00:10:42.349
on lots of different pages that
we scan, is this means A, B, C.

00:10:42.349 --> 00:10:43.140
Does it make sense?

00:10:43.140 --> 00:10:46.220
And we can also tell you
if a similar image exists

00:10:46.220 --> 00:10:47.570
somewhere else on the web.

00:10:47.570 --> 00:10:49.417
So that's another
aspect that we provide.

00:10:49.417 --> 00:10:51.000
So really, the
scenario we think about

00:10:51.000 --> 00:10:53.130
is if it's topical entities,
where it's now here,

00:10:53.130 --> 00:10:56.370
for example, you want to find
a product with a very famous

00:10:56.370 --> 00:10:59.190
logo, we can pick
up that character,

00:10:59.190 --> 00:11:02.640
like a Marvel character or
a character from a BBC show

00:11:02.640 --> 00:11:04.800
that's very popular in the web.

00:11:04.800 --> 00:11:08.280
We most probably would pick up
that character from an image.

00:11:08.280 --> 00:11:12.032
And the other aspect is also is
for fraudulent scenarios, where

00:11:12.032 --> 00:11:13.740
if you're a marketplace,
you want to see,

00:11:13.740 --> 00:11:16.950
are these people posting
fraudulent images or stock

00:11:16.950 --> 00:11:18.155
images on the marketplace?

00:11:18.155 --> 00:11:20.030
All right, so we'll all
do it out of the box.

00:11:20.030 --> 00:11:22.110
And I'll show you
some examples of that.

00:11:22.110 --> 00:11:25.830
But be careful about this.

00:11:25.830 --> 00:11:27.600
Because we use the
web metadata, we

00:11:27.600 --> 00:11:29.790
can get a lot of
false positives too.

00:11:29.790 --> 00:11:32.910
So for an example of this,
this is clearly a Stormtrooper

00:11:32.910 --> 00:11:36.360
image, but we also give you
a metadata called pregnancy.

00:11:36.360 --> 00:11:38.590
And you're like, why
did you get that?

00:11:38.590 --> 00:11:41.130
So if you click on
this link, it actually

00:11:41.130 --> 00:11:42.900
goes to a Pinterest page.

00:11:42.900 --> 00:11:44.490
And somebody there
has used this image

00:11:44.490 --> 00:11:47.457
to drive a baby shower, right?

00:11:47.457 --> 00:11:48.540
And so that's [INAUDIBLE].

00:11:48.540 --> 00:11:51.010
So very clearly, it's a
very popular pinned page.

00:11:51.010 --> 00:11:53.370
And so a lot of pages
are linked to it.

00:11:53.370 --> 00:11:54.570
And that drives our ranking.

00:11:54.570 --> 00:11:57.692
And it clearly was on this
image of a Stormtrooper helmet.

00:11:57.692 --> 00:11:58.900
It's now linked to pregnancy.

00:11:58.900 --> 00:12:00.750
So you just need
to think through.

00:12:00.750 --> 00:12:03.810
I just want to caution
you, this is not a antidote

00:12:03.810 --> 00:12:04.970
to all problems.

00:12:04.970 --> 00:12:06.780
It works well for
many scenarios,

00:12:06.780 --> 00:12:09.300
but also doesn't work
well for some scenarios.

00:12:09.300 --> 00:12:12.060
So that's a piece that I
wanted to leave you guys with.

00:12:12.060 --> 00:12:14.490
So let's kind of walk through
how you want to do that.

00:12:14.490 --> 00:12:15.858
Can you switch to a demo?

