00:16:02.441 --> 00:16:03.415
Back to slide.

00:16:07.467 --> 00:16:09.050
So let's move on to
the next use case.

00:16:09.050 --> 00:16:10.508
We're talking about
recommendation.

00:16:10.508 --> 00:16:12.420
So in the case of
recommendation,

00:16:12.420 --> 00:16:13.920
there's a lot of
different scenarios

00:16:13.920 --> 00:16:15.270
that we want to talk about.

00:16:15.270 --> 00:16:17.044
Really, you can
think about it drives

00:16:17.044 --> 00:16:19.710
a lot of different scenarios all
the way from thinking about how

00:16:19.710 --> 00:16:22.120
to view the right
content in YouTube

00:16:22.120 --> 00:16:24.720
to thinking about how you can
write appropriate cross-sell

00:16:24.720 --> 00:16:25.920
or upsell for the customer.

00:16:25.920 --> 00:16:28.170
But as I mentioned, there's
a lot of different factors

00:16:28.170 --> 00:16:30.450
that drive into the
recommendation aspect.

00:16:30.450 --> 00:16:32.080
So you really want
to think about,

00:16:32.080 --> 00:16:34.124
what's the history
of the user behavior?

00:16:34.124 --> 00:16:35.790
What has a user been
doing on your site?

00:16:35.790 --> 00:16:38.760
Or what purchases have they done
with your store prior to that?

00:16:38.760 --> 00:16:40.500
What's the current
product metadata?

00:16:40.500 --> 00:16:42.510
That's really where,
if you can imagine,

00:16:42.510 --> 00:16:44.550
if your product is more
of a physical device,

00:16:44.550 --> 00:16:47.077
your product is more of a soft
device, whether it's a video

00:16:47.077 --> 00:16:48.660
or it's a piece of
content, you really

00:16:48.660 --> 00:16:51.940
want to get that metadata, so
you can drive things downstream

00:16:51.940 --> 00:16:53.550
around recommendation models.

00:16:53.550 --> 00:16:55.591
And Rajiv's going to talk
a little bit about some

00:16:55.591 --> 00:16:58.470
of the models that enable that.

00:16:58.470 --> 00:17:00.960
We provide you a rich
platform to enable that.

00:17:00.960 --> 00:17:02.931
And so right now, what
we use is TensorFlow.

00:17:02.931 --> 00:17:05.430
So [INAUDIBLE] really when you
think about machine learning,

00:17:05.430 --> 00:17:07.079
there is a pipeline for that.

00:17:07.079 --> 00:17:09.450
There's a pipeline for really
thinking about training,

00:17:09.450 --> 00:17:12.480
how you think about training
this model, where you take

00:17:12.480 --> 00:17:15.109
your data, the data
gets the data in GCS,

00:17:15.109 --> 00:17:18.089
in Google Cloud Storage.

00:17:18.089 --> 00:17:20.190
You feed it through
a data flow pipeline

00:17:20.190 --> 00:17:23.490
to process the data to get the
right features that you want.

00:17:23.490 --> 00:17:25.241
Then you get a
TensorFlow model that you

00:17:25.241 --> 00:17:27.490
have gone through training,
that you look at the model

00:17:27.490 --> 00:17:29.620
parameters, tweak
it appropriately,

00:17:29.620 --> 00:17:32.130
and then the model goes
into production, which then

00:17:32.130 --> 00:17:34.010
drives downstream processes.

00:17:34.010 --> 00:17:36.506
So this is really where
TensorFlow and things

00:17:36.506 --> 00:17:38.880
like cloud ML Engine comes
into play to really drive this

00:17:38.880 --> 00:17:41.610
into a model scenario.

00:17:41.610 --> 00:17:43.285
But then you can
imagine, the pipeline

00:17:43.285 --> 00:17:44.910
doesn't just sit with
machine learning.

00:17:44.910 --> 00:17:46.034
The pipeline is end to end.

00:17:46.034 --> 00:17:48.060
The pipeline goes
all the way from how

00:17:48.060 --> 00:17:50.400
you think about your logs
data, your clickstream

00:17:50.400 --> 00:17:52.170
behavior coming in
from-- whether it's

00:17:52.170 --> 00:17:55.170
cloud Pub/Sub, whether it's
metadata flowing through things

00:17:55.170 --> 00:17:57.019
like Cloud Storage.

00:17:57.019 --> 00:17:58.560
And the end result
is really thinking

00:17:58.560 --> 00:18:01.410
about all your analysts who
have to do things around hey,

00:18:01.410 --> 00:18:03.840
help me understand
my prior history,

00:18:03.840 --> 00:18:06.150
my sales behavior,
my purchase history.

00:18:06.150 --> 00:18:08.610
And thinking about how to
then figure out all the BI

00:18:08.610 --> 00:18:10.440
and data warehousing
stuff that's

00:18:10.440 --> 00:18:12.210
been going on for 10, 15 years.

00:18:12.210 --> 00:18:15.290
All those things does not go
away with machine learning.

00:18:15.290 --> 00:18:18.330
You can imagine, you need that
to drive machine learning.

00:18:18.330 --> 00:18:21.630
If you don't have that, go
back and fix your BI and data

00:18:21.630 --> 00:18:22.400
warehouses.

00:18:22.400 --> 00:18:24.540
Because without data,
there is no ML, right?

00:18:24.540 --> 00:18:26.190
So the dirty secret
of machine learning

00:18:26.190 --> 00:18:29.760
is 90% of the work is getting
your data into your data

00:18:29.760 --> 00:18:31.480
warehouse, cleansed,
processed, so you

00:18:31.480 --> 00:18:33.480
can drive downstream
machine learning processes.

00:18:33.480 --> 00:18:35.490
So we shouldn't
forget that piece.

00:18:35.490 --> 00:18:38.260
Think of it as a tip
of the spear, right?

00:18:38.260 --> 00:18:40.170
It's a shiny part, but
there's a lot of work

00:18:40.170 --> 00:18:42.990
that happens to get that data
to the model for training.

00:18:42.990 --> 00:18:44.670
So this kind of
scenario kind of talks

00:18:44.670 --> 00:18:47.100
about the end-to-end
pipeline, not just from how

00:18:47.100 --> 00:18:50.300
do we feed data, whether think
about clickstream behavior,

00:18:50.300 --> 00:18:53.790
these are lots of events that
goes across all your servers,

00:18:53.790 --> 00:18:56.700
feeding through a cloud Pub/Sub
pipeline that's integrated

00:18:56.700 --> 00:18:58.710
with data flow that
does your processing,

00:18:58.710 --> 00:19:00.499
then dumps your
data into BigQuery

00:19:00.499 --> 00:19:02.040
where your analyst
most probably will

00:19:02.040 --> 00:19:04.290
run a bunch of
queries and analysis

00:19:04.290 --> 00:19:07.519
on that with your favorite BI
tool, whether that's Tableau.

00:19:07.519 --> 00:19:09.810
And then once you've kind of
done your data processing,

00:19:09.810 --> 00:19:11.760
what they call the
data janitorial work,

00:19:11.760 --> 00:19:13.885
where you figured out what's
the right data, what's

00:19:13.885 --> 00:19:16.410
the right features, you then
do subsequent processing,

00:19:16.410 --> 00:19:20.040
feed that into a format that's
compatible with TensorFlow,

00:19:20.040 --> 00:19:22.000
create your cloud
machine learning model,

00:19:22.000 --> 00:19:23.580
create a TensorFlow
model, and then

00:19:23.580 --> 00:19:26.370
you do some appropriate
level of evaluation,

00:19:26.370 --> 00:19:28.620
then put it in production,
most probably not directly

00:19:28.620 --> 00:19:29.530
in production.

00:19:29.530 --> 00:19:31.867
It will have to do things
like an A/B test process.

00:19:31.867 --> 00:19:33.450
We figure out if
this the right model.

00:19:33.450 --> 00:19:36.420
Am I getting the results I
want in actual production

00:19:36.420 --> 00:19:38.360
before you roll it out, right?

00:19:38.360 --> 00:19:40.170
There's a lot of
processes that happen

00:19:40.170 --> 00:19:47.580
before user behavior data
goes to an actual ML model.

00:19:47.580 --> 00:19:49.363
What I want to hand
it off to Rajiv

00:19:49.363 --> 00:19:51.330
is to talk about
how someone actually

00:19:51.330 --> 00:19:52.306
did this in production.

00:19:52.306 --> 00:19:54.180
And Rajiv is going to
walk through a use case

00:19:54.180 --> 00:19:56.506
where he's helped the customer
walk through the scenario end

00:19:56.506 --> 00:19:57.005
to end.

