00:22:24.360 --> 00:22:28.710
And all the app code is
driven on App Engine.

00:22:28.710 --> 00:22:31.800
And we basically
have the input data

00:22:31.800 --> 00:22:34.440
coming when a user
selects different TV shows

00:22:34.440 --> 00:22:37.360
that he or she
likes, and that goes

00:22:37.360 --> 00:22:40.590
into a single-layered
neural network

00:22:40.590 --> 00:22:44.220
that we've built and
deployed on TensorFlow.

00:22:44.220 --> 00:22:48.930
And I'll show you some screens
and graphs from that as well.

00:22:48.930 --> 00:22:52.680
And based on that,
the TensorFlow

00:22:52.680 --> 00:22:58.570
figures out what products should
be recommended for that person.

00:22:58.570 --> 00:23:01.290
Essentially, the
math challenge here

00:23:01.290 --> 00:23:05.190
is, how do you do good
filtering for a user

00:23:05.190 --> 00:23:07.890
without having a lot of
data about that user?

00:23:07.890 --> 00:23:12.320
There's a lot of different
types of filtering you can do.

00:23:12.320 --> 00:23:14.680
Essentially, they
all have fancy names.

00:23:14.680 --> 00:23:16.680
But basically, it
comes down to, do

00:23:16.680 --> 00:23:19.920
you want to filter stuff
based on what this user has

00:23:19.920 --> 00:23:25.060
done in the past or based
on if I like to watch HBO,

00:23:25.060 --> 00:23:26.850
somebody else
likes to watch HBO,

00:23:26.850 --> 00:23:29.040
and that somebody
else also likes

00:23:29.040 --> 00:23:33.180
to watch Comedy
Central, then what's

00:23:33.180 --> 00:23:37.290
the likelihood I would like to
watch Comedy Central as well?

00:23:37.290 --> 00:23:41.700
And there's different types of
filtering techniques like that.

00:23:41.700 --> 00:23:45.680
We've picked a collaborative
filtering technique here.

00:23:45.680 --> 00:23:48.720
What it basically means is
if you have all your users

00:23:48.720 --> 00:23:53.070
on one axis and you've all
your shows or TV packages

00:23:53.070 --> 00:23:56.310
on the other axis,
you have some data

00:23:56.310 --> 00:23:59.250
for users for some
of the packages

00:23:59.250 --> 00:24:01.350
and you have holes in places.

00:24:01.350 --> 00:24:03.210
And so the whole
mathematical challenge

00:24:03.210 --> 00:24:04.830
is, how do you fill the holes?

00:24:04.830 --> 00:24:09.960
How do you know that if I've
picked A, B, and C, then

00:24:09.960 --> 00:24:12.990
what's the likelihood I'd
be interested in D as well?

00:24:12.990 --> 00:24:17.370
And the reason we use
TensorFlow for this

00:24:17.370 --> 00:24:21.960
is our initial training data
had something like 20,000 users.

00:24:21.960 --> 00:24:28.620
And on the other axis, we
had some data for these users

00:24:28.620 --> 00:24:31.710
on about 300 different
types of TV channels.

00:24:31.710 --> 00:24:36.300
So essentially, it was
like a 20,000 by 300 matrix

00:24:36.300 --> 00:24:38.400
that we were executing.

00:24:38.400 --> 00:24:41.850
But that pretty soon
exploded into having

00:24:41.850 --> 00:24:46.980
over two million users and
something like 20,000 data

00:24:46.980 --> 00:24:48.100
points.

00:24:48.100 --> 00:24:51.670
And so that becomes a very
hard computational problem.

00:24:51.670 --> 00:24:57.150
And that's where you can easily
tweak your models in TensorFlow

00:24:57.150 --> 00:25:01.050
to scale with the amount
of data that you get.

