00:29:15.330 --> 00:29:17.410
RAJIV SALIMATH: Great.

00:29:17.410 --> 00:29:21.760
So I'm not sure how many of
you know a lot about this

00:29:21.760 --> 00:29:24.040
and are going to grill me
about that, how many of you

00:29:24.040 --> 00:29:26.050
don't know much at
all about TensorFlow.

00:29:26.050 --> 00:29:31.510
So I'll briefly go
over some things.

00:29:31.510 --> 00:29:36.250
So essentially, TensorFlow is
a computation engine, right?

00:29:36.250 --> 00:29:38.140
And you give it
some model and it's

00:29:38.140 --> 00:29:39.790
going to execute it for you.

00:29:39.790 --> 00:29:42.790
And how you design that
model, which in this case,

00:29:42.790 --> 00:29:46.720
happens to be a
single-layer neural network.

00:29:46.720 --> 00:29:52.120
And when you feed it, so you see
this thing called Softmax here.

00:29:52.120 --> 00:29:55.990
So that's kind of the
function that we use.

00:29:55.990 --> 00:29:59.860
And that decides kind of
the bias and the weight.

00:29:59.860 --> 00:30:03.820
So if you think about all
the users on one axis and all

00:30:03.820 --> 00:30:07.210
the interests on another axis,
let's say, hypothetically,

00:30:07.210 --> 00:30:13.950
it's a 20,000-by-200
matrix, right?

00:30:13.950 --> 00:30:18.070
And out of that,
that's my input tensor.

00:30:18.070 --> 00:30:22.150
And that has to give me
an output that tells me

00:30:22.150 --> 00:30:26.290
for each of these 20,000 users,
which one of the 10 television

00:30:26.290 --> 00:30:28.760
packages they can sell to me.

00:30:28.760 --> 00:30:34.330
So the 20,000-by-200 input
training tensor should give me

00:30:34.330 --> 00:30:42.340
an output of 20,000
by 10, essentially.

00:30:42.340 --> 00:30:45.280
And to do that, essentially,
you have to do some kind of--

00:30:45.280 --> 00:30:48.310
if you were to do it without
something like TensorFlow,

00:30:48.310 --> 00:30:52.120
you'd sit and compute different
permutations and combinations

00:30:52.120 --> 00:30:55.240
of the weights that you have
to put into this and the bias

00:30:55.240 --> 00:30:56.570
that you have to put into this.

00:30:56.570 --> 00:30:59.830
So the beauty of
TensorFlow is that it

00:30:59.830 --> 00:31:02.200
allows you to define
a function and play

00:31:02.200 --> 00:31:06.340
with the bias and the
weight in rapid time

00:31:06.340 --> 00:31:10.980
till you can get your
input model to a level

00:31:10.980 --> 00:31:16.290
where it looks like you're
getting the right outputs

00:31:16.290 --> 00:31:18.310
from the training set.

00:31:18.310 --> 00:31:21.220
So when you do that,
when you have that model,

00:31:21.220 --> 00:31:25.770
and then if you look
at the distribution,

00:31:25.770 --> 00:31:29.650
essentially, over time, it
has to kind of converge,

00:31:29.650 --> 00:31:32.580
so you know that the
bias that you've given

00:31:32.580 --> 00:31:35.710
or the weight that you're put
into your weighted algorithm,

00:31:35.710 --> 00:31:40.600
it's kind of coming to
a sort of a convergence,

00:31:40.600 --> 00:31:43.380
so you know how good or
bad your training model is.

00:31:46.090 --> 00:31:48.300
And I'll show you
one last thing maybe.

00:31:48.300 --> 00:31:52.900
So if you're doing
anything with TensorFlow

00:31:52.900 --> 00:31:56.200
and you've always got somebody
yelling at you, saying,

00:31:56.200 --> 00:31:57.850
how do I know how
good or bad this is?

00:31:57.850 --> 00:31:59.890
How do I know how
accurate this is?

00:31:59.890 --> 00:32:03.670
And you kind of have to look
at a loss curve over time

00:32:03.670 --> 00:32:05.770
to kind of say,
hey, you know, it

00:32:05.770 --> 00:32:08.810
has to go down a
little bit over time,

00:32:08.810 --> 00:32:12.510
even if it doesn't
go down this much.

00:32:12.510 --> 00:32:16.240
If it's not going down and that
last curve is not reducing,

00:32:16.240 --> 00:32:19.060
then the variance
is too high and you

00:32:19.060 --> 00:32:21.690
have to go back and kind of
tweak your model some more.

00:32:21.690 --> 00:32:24.970
So I just wanted to show
you a couple screens.

00:32:24.970 --> 00:32:27.280
And TensorFlow
makes it really easy

00:32:27.280 --> 00:32:31.500
to do those things
on TensorBoard

00:32:31.500 --> 00:32:36.010
and execute the functions
on TensorFlow instead

00:32:36.010 --> 00:32:39.130
of having to kind
of go through lots

00:32:39.130 --> 00:32:45.640
of permutations of iterations
for those input functions.

00:32:45.640 --> 00:32:48.652
So that's what we did with
one of our clients, Sky.

